# systemd-networkd configuration template - MULE
#
# Link configuration, addressing and mesh attachment. FML-ADR-059 makes
# systemd-networkd the owner of all three and forbids a second general manager:
# NetworkManager and ifupdown are not installed. Association stays with
# wpa_supplicant and hostapd, because no link configuration component performs
# it.
#
# STATUS: TEMPLATE. Every value marked TBD is undecided. This file describes the
# file set a node needs and what each one must contain. It is an input to
# configuration generation, not a file to be edited on a node, and networkd
# reads none of it in this form: the real thing is several files under
# /etc/systemd/network/ and this is what generates them.
#
# Trades: TBR-LINUX-01 (interface naming, driver bring-up behaviour)
#         TBR-NET-01   (field address prefix and allocation)
#         TBR-RF-01    (whether the high-rate bearer joins the same mesh)
#         TBR-RF-03    (whether the access point shares a physical radio)
#
# THE ORDER IS mule/bringup.py AND IS NOT RESTATED HERE. That module holds it as
# constraint pairs with their sources. What this file records is how networkd
# expresses it, which is a different question and mostly a matter of which
# setting goes in which file.

# --- What networkd owns, and what it does not -------------------------------
#
# Owns:  the batadv device, its algorithm and its tunables; which links are
#        members of the mesh; MTU; the field bridge; addressing.
# Does not own:  802.11s mesh point creation and association, which is
#        wpa_supplicant; the access point, which is hostapd.
#
# THE SEAM BETWEEN THOSE TWO IS WHERE THIS GOES WRONG. networkd cannot create
# an 802.11s mesh point interface. A hard interface must exist and be
# associated before networkd may add it to the mesh, and batman-adv attached to
# an interface that is not yet up fails in a way that LOOKS LIKE A RADIO FAULT.
#
# CORRECTED 2026-08-30: networkd already handles this and an earlier version of
# this paragraph said it did not. systemd.network(5) on
# ConfigureWithoutCarrier=: "Allows systemd-networkd to configure a specific
# link even if it has no carrier. Defaults to false." A .network file with
# BatmanAdvanced= therefore does not apply until the interface has carrier,
# which a mesh point does not have until wpa_supplicant has joined the mesh.
#
# So there is no ordering unit to write. There is a setting NOT to change:
# DO NOT set ConfigureWithoutCarrier=true on a mesh member. It turns the
# hazard back on, and it looks like a fix to someone debugging a link that is
# slow to come up. See os/config/systemd-units.template.
#
# Verified 2026-08-30 on mac80211_hwsim: a mesh point reports carrier 0 before
# joining and 1 after. What remains unverified is whether a real driver does
# the same, which is a narrower question and cheap to check on a board. See
# docs/evidence/TBR-LINUX-01/2026-08-30-80211s-mesh-in-software.md.

# --- 10-bat0.netdev ---------------------------------------------------------
# The mesh interface itself. Created by networkd, not by batctl: FML-ADR-059.
#
# [NetDev]
# Name=TBD                     # e.g. bat0. TBR-LINUX-01 for naming rules.
# Kind=batadv
#
# [BatmanAdvanced]
# RoutingAlgorithm=batman-iv
#
#   FML-ADR-053 selects BATMAN-IV. networkd spells it batman-iv; batctl and the
#   module parameter spell the same algorithm BATMAN_IV.
#
#   **networkd DEFAULTS THIS TO batman-v.** Omitting the line does not give you
#   the module's default of BATMAN_IV; it gives you the algorithm FML-ADR-053
#   rejected. That is a silent trap on the baseline OS, because Debian 13's
#   module supports BATMAN_V, so the node comes up, forms a mesh, and works,
#   while running the superseded decision. See
#   docs/evidence/TBR-LINUX-01/2026-08-30-batman-v-available-on-debian.md.
#
#   It is set HERE, on the netdev, and that is the whole reason this is not a
#   script. systemd.netdev(5): "The algorithm cannot be changed after interface
#   creation." Not after the first member is added, after the DEVICE is
#   created, which is earlier than the constraint mule/bringup.py used to
#   carry. Expressing it where the device is defined removes the hazard rather
#   than requiring a unit to respect it.
#
# BridgeLoopAvoidance=false
#
#   THIS STAYS OFF, PERMANENTLY, and the same standing note is in
#   os/config/batman-adv.conf.template. The loop it protects against is
#   prevented another way: FML-ADR-056 forbids the bridge carrying the mesh
#   interface from holding anything but EUD access points. Turning it on costs
#   30 seconds of total silence on every mesh formation while every diagnostic
#   table reads correct, which this program has diagnosed from scratch twice.
#
#   FML-ADR-056. Disabled deliberately, and the ADR asks for a loop detector in
#   exchange: a client address under more than one originator, or the node's own
#   bridge address arriving from the mesh. Neither is implemented, and this
#   template cannot implement them. See docs/ROADMAP-DEV.md item 1.2.
#
#   **networkd DEFAULTS THIS TO true.** Omitting the line enables the thing
#   FML-ADR-056 disables. Both settings in this section default the wrong way
#   for this program, which is worth knowing before anyone trims the file.
#
# Other [BatmanAdvanced] settings this node may need are TBD and belong to
# os/config/batman-adv.conf.template, which lists them with their trades:
# OriginatorIntervalSec, HopPenalty, GatewayMode, DistributedArpTable,
# Fragmentation.

# --- 20-mesh-member.network -------------------------------------------------
# A hard interface that carries the mesh. One file per member.
#
# [Match]
# Name=TBD                     # TBR-LINUX-01. Do not match a kernel-assigned
#                              # name; use a stable rule.
#
# [Link]
# MTUBytes=1560
#
#   Not derived. batman-adv prints it when the hard interface is too small:
#   "Setting the MTU to 1560 would solve the problem." Set here rather than
#   after attachment, for the same reason as the algorithm above.
#
# [Network]
# BatmanAdvanced=TBD           # the bat0 device named in 10-bat0.netdev
#
#   A MEMBER INTERFACE CARRIES NO IP ADDRESS. The mesh interface does.
#   Configuring an address here is a common error with confusing symptoms; see
#   os/config/interfaces.conf.template.

# --- 30-bat0.network --------------------------------------------------------
# The mesh interface: addressing, and whether it joins the field bridge.
#
# [Match]
# Name=TBD                     # the bat0 device
#
# [Network]
# Address=TBD                  # TBR-NET-01. Family, prefix source and host
#                              # allocation are all open. An address derived
#                              # from a durable node identity IS a durable
#                              # identifier; see THREAT_MODEL.md.
# Bridge=TBD                   # the field bridge, if SAD section 4.3's model
#                              # is used. FML-ADR-056 constrains what else may
#                              # share it.

# --- 40-field-bridge.netdev and the access point -----------------------------
# SAD section 4.3 bridges local EUD access into the BATMAN domain so peer ATAK
# multicast traverses the mesh and clients need no MANET routing awareness.
#
# [NetDev]
# Name=TBD                     # e.g. br-field
# Kind=bridge
#
# WHAT MAY SHARE THIS BRIDGE IS FML-ADR-056 AND IS NOT A FREE CHOICE. The
# access point may. An uplink may not: two nodes bridging one segment form a
# loop, and bridge loop avoidance is disabled above, so nothing is left to
# break it. A wired link carrying field traffic joins the MESH with
# BatmanAdvanced=, not the bridge.
#
# tools/validate-docs.sh check 19 enforces that rule, and CANNOT SEE IT HERE.
# It reads bridge membership from a single line, and networkd states membership
# one file per member, so the mesh interface and an uplink never appear
# together for it to match. The gap is recorded in that check and is the reason
# this template states the rule in prose: for now a reader is the enforcement.

# --- What is deliberately absent ---------------------------------------------
#
# The LoRa plane. FML-ADR-026 makes it a separate NON-IP plane: no address, no
# bridge, no place in IP routing, and nothing for networkd to own. Its
# configuration is the daemon's own and it needs a supervisor, because a
# configuration change reboots meshtasticd and the re-exec fails in at least
# one image; see .github/workflows/lora-probe.yml. A configuration push that
# leaves the lifeline bearer dead is the worst failure this system has.
#
# Region-dependent values. No frequency, channel, bandwidth or transmit power
# appears in this file or in any file in this directory. They come from a
# region profile under regions/<region-id>/. See os/config/README.md and
# REGULATORY.md.
